BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[ATH] Fall in Love With the Classics at Holbrook Palmer Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T113000
DTSTAMP:20260904T164637Z
UID:smcl-6a04db440c0f2c4503fa0735@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Join this fall for a three-part book discussion series explori
 ng love\, ambition\, and social expectations through classic literature. T
 his limited-run series invites readers to examine how relationships and cl
 ass shape personal choices.\n\nWe’ll meet monthly from August through No
 vember on the third Wednesday of the month for welcoming and engaging conv
 ersations. Books will be provided. Attendance at each discussion meeting 
 is required in order to receive the next book in the series.\n\n\nJoin us 
 for our discussion of Elizabeth Gaskell’s North and South. Together we
 ’ll explore the novel’s themes of class\, industry\, social conflict\,
  and the evolving relationship between Margaret Hale and John Thornton.\n
 \nDuring this meeting\, participants will receive the third and final book
  of the series\, Edith Wharton’s The House of Mirth. Set in New York’s
  glittering high society\, the novel follows Lily Bart as she struggles to
  balance personal independence\, love\, and the pressures of wealth and st
 atus. Wharton’s sharp portrayal of ambition and social expectations rema
 ins both elegant and deeply moving.\n\nThe book club will meet at: North M
 eadow Picnic Tables at Holbrook Palmer Park.\n\nAges: Adult program (kids 
 welcome to tag along\, not designed for them)\nRegistration: none\nSource:
  https://smcl.bibliocommons.com/events/6a04db440c0f2c4503fa0735\nLast sync
 ed: 2026-09-04 09:46 PDT
LOCATION:Atherton Library\, 2 Dinkelspiel Station Ln\, Atherton\, CA 94027
URL:https://smcl.bibliocommons.com/events/6a04db440c0f2c4503fa0735
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[ATH] Block Prints: Nahuatl Hieroglyphs of the Mexika Calendar
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260923T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260923T130000
DTSTAMP:20260904T164637Z
UID:smcl-6a9a6ae129a7dd0d6748ff0f@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Participants carve and print one of the 20 Mexika (meh-SHEE-ka
 h) daysigns of the tonalpohualli\, a solar-based calendar that correlates 
 with the Mayan calendar and that of other Indigenous cultures of present-d
 ay Mexico and Central America. It is also closely aligned to the highly ac
 curate Persian calendar used in Iran and Afghanistan\, because it also beg
 ins the day after the spring equinox (March 20 or 21 on the Gregorian Cale
 ndar developed in Europe.) The Mexika people\, commonly known as "Aztecs\,
 " founded the city of Mexico-Tenochtitlan\, which was replaced by Mexico C
 ity and gave Mexico its name. The Mexika wrote their language\, Nahuatl (N
 AH-waht) in glyphic script\, hieroglyphs\, symbols and pictograms to repre
 sent sounds\, ideas\, and objects.\n\nDue to high demand\, we ask that com
 munity members sign up for a single occurrence of this series so that as m
 any community members as possible can participate in this experience.\n\nA
 ges: Adult program (kids welcome to tag along\, not designed for them)\nRe
 gistration: required\nSource: https://smcl.bibliocommons.com/events/6a9a6a
 e129a7dd0d6748ff0f\nLast synced: 2026-09-04 09:46 PDT
LOCATION:Atherton Library\, 2 Dinkelspiel Station Ln\, Atherton\, CA 94027
URL:https://smcl.bibliocommons.com/events/6a9a6ae129a7dd0d6748ff0f
END:VEVENT
